@page
@model EduHelpdesk.Pages.Settings.SignInModel
@using EduHelpdesk.Services
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Sign-in security";
    var withTwoFactor = Model.Staff.Count(x => x.TwoFactor is not null);
}
<div class="page-heading"><div><h1>Sign-in security</h1><p class="hero-copy">How technician accounts are protected. The staff portal has its own passwords and isn't covered by two-step sign-in.</p></div></div>
@if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Message)) { <div class="notice">@Model.Message</div> }

<section class="panel">
    <h2>Two-step sign-in <help-tip key="signin.two-step" /></h2>
    <p>@withTwoFactor of @Model.Staff.Count active technician account@(Model.Staff.Count == 1 ? " has" : "s have") it set up. The DfE's cyber security standard for schools expects it on accounts like these, which can change anything in the helpdesk.</p>
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Require" data-confirm="@(Model.Required ? "Make two-step sign-in optional? Those who have it keep it." : "Require two-step sign-in for every technician account? Anyone without it will have to set it up before they can do anything else.")">
        <input type="hidden" name="required" value="@(Model.Required ? "false" : "true")" />
        <p>@(Model.Required ? "It is required: anyone without it sets it up the next time they use the helpdesk." : "It is each person's choice at the moment.")</p>
        <button class="button @(Model.Required ? "button-secondary" : "button-primary")">@(Model.Required ? "Make it optional" : "Require it for everyone")</button>
    </form>
    <div class="table-wrap"><table class="stack-table"><thead><tr><th data-stack="head">Technician account</th><th>Role</th><th>Two-step sign-in</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    @foreach (var person in Model.Staff)
    {
        <tr>
            <td>@if (Model.CanEditStaff) { <a asp-page="/People/Technician" asp-route-id="@person.Id">@person.Name</a> } else { @person.Name }<br /><span class="muted">@person.Email</span></td>
            <td>@person.Role</td>
            <td>@if (person.TwoFactor is { } twoFactor) { <span class="badge">On since @twoFactor.EnabledAt.ToLocalTime().ToString("d MMM yyyy")</span> } else { <span class="badge @(Model.Required ? "badge-warning" : "")">Not set up</span> }</td>
        </tr>
    }
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="muted">Someone who has lost their phone and their recovery codes: open their technician account and reset it, then they set it up again.</p>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="recovery">
    <h2>Recovery keys <help-tip key="signin.recovery" /></h2>
    <p>@Model.Staff.Count(x => x.RecoveryKey is not null) of @Model.Staff.Count active technician account@(Model.Staff.Count == 1 ? " has" : "s have") a recovery key. With one, a technician who forgets their password can set a new one from the sign-in page, once, without waiting for an administrator. Two-step sign-in still applies when they sign in.</p>
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="RecoveryKeys" data-confirm="@(Model.RecoveryKeysAllowed ? "Turn recovery keys off? Keys people have made are kept, but stop working until you turn them back on." : "Let technicians reset a forgotten password with a recovery key?")">
        <input type="hidden" name="allowed" value="@(Model.RecoveryKeysAllowed ? "false" : "true")" />
        <p>@(Model.RecoveryKeysAllowed ? "They are allowed: each technician can make one from Recovery key in their account menu." : "They are turned off: a forgotten password has to be reset by an administrator.")</p>
        <button class="button button-secondary">@(Model.RecoveryKeysAllowed ? "Turn recovery keys off" : "Allow recovery keys")</button>
    </form>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="phones">
    <h2>Phone app <help-tip key="signin.phones" /></h2>
    <p>The EduHelpdesk app lets technicians see their tickets, take them with Assign to me or On my way, and log new ones from an Android phone. Each phone is paired from <a asp-page="/Phones">Phones</a> in the account menu, can do only what the person's role allows, and can be revoked there.</p>
    @if (!Model.IsHttps) { <div class="notice notice-warning">The app only connects over HTTPS, and this page came over plain HTTP. Turn HTTPS on before turning the app on: run Enable-Https.cmd from the folder the helpdesk was installed from.</div> }
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="PhoneApp" data-confirm="@(Model.PhoneAppAllowed ? "Turn the phone app off? Paired phones stop working until it is turned back on." : "Let technicians use the phone app?")">
        <input type="hidden" name="allowed" value="@(Model.PhoneAppAllowed ? "false" : "true")" />
        <p>@(Model.PhoneAppAllowed ? $"It is on. {Model.PairedPhones} phone{(Model.PairedPhones == 1 ? " is" : "s are")} paired." : "It is off.")
            @(Model.PhoneRoles.Count == 0 ? "No role has Use the phone app ticked yet, so nobody can pair a phone." : $"Roles with Use the phone app: {string.Join(", ", Model.PhoneRoles)}.")
            @if (Model.CanEditRoles) { <a asp-page="/People" asp-route-tab="roles">Edit roles</a> }</p>
        <button class="button @(Model.PhoneAppAllowed ? "button-secondary" : "button-primary")">@(Model.PhoneAppAllowed ? "Turn the phone app off" : "Turn the phone app on")</button>
    </form>
    <h3>App lock</h3>
    <p>With app lock, the app asks for the phone's fingerprint, face or PIN when it has been away for 5 minutes. @(Model.PhoneAppLockRequired ? "It is required on every phone." : "Each technician chooses for their own phone.")</p>
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="PhoneAppLock">
        <input type="hidden" name="required" value="@(Model.PhoneAppLockRequired ? "false" : "true")" />
        <button class="button button-secondary">@(Model.PhoneAppLockRequired ? "Let technicians choose" : "Require app lock on phones")</button>
    </form>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="address">
    <h2>Helpdesk address</h2>
    <p>The address staff type to reach the helpdesk, printed on the quick start guide given to each new account. Leave it empty to use the address the guide is printed from - at the moment that would be <strong>@Model.GuessedAddress</strong>.</p>
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Address" class="settings-form field-narrow">
        <label><span>Address <help-tip key="signin.address" /></span><input name="address" type="url" value="@Model.SiteAddress" placeholder="https://helpdesk.school.org.uk/" maxlength="200" /></label>
        <button class="button button-primary">Save address</button>
    </form>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="dashboard">
    <h2>Shown inside EduDashboard</h2>
    <p>Off unless you give an address. The helpdesk then refuses to be shown inside any other site, which stops a page elsewhere passing itself off as the helpdesk to trick someone into clicking. To let the school's EduDashboard show the helpdesk as a tile inside it, give the dashboard's address: only that site may, and nothing else changes.</p>
    @foreach (var warning in Model.DashboardWarnings) { <div class="notice notice-warning">@warning</div> }
    <form method="post" asp-page-handler="Dashboard" class="settings-form field-narrow">
        <label><span>Dashboard address <help-tip key="signin.dashboard" /></span><input name="address" type="url" value="@Model.DashboardAddress" placeholder="https://dashboard.school.org.uk" maxlength="200" spellcheck="false" /></label>
        <button class="button button-primary">Save dashboard address</button>
    </form>
    <p class="muted">Keep the helpdesk and the dashboard on the same domain - such as helpdesk.school.org.uk and dashboard.school.org.uk, or one server on two ports - or browsers may not keep technicians signed in inside the dashboard. On the dashboard's tile, choose <strong>Show inside the dashboard</strong> and press <strong>Test</strong>.</p>
</section>

<section class="panel">
    <h2>Passwords and lockouts</h2>
    <dl class="detail-list">
        <dt>Encryption</dt><dd>@(Model.IsHttps ? "Passwords reach the helpdesk encrypted (HTTPS)." : "This page came over plain HTTP, so passwords cross the network unencrypted. Run Enable-Https.cmd from the folder the helpdesk was installed from: it asks whether to make a self-signed certificate or use one you already have. See the Install Guide.")</dd>
        <dt>Passwords</dt><dd>At least @PasswordRules.MinimumLength characters, and not one of the passwords guessed first or the person's own name.</dd>
        <dt>New accounts</dt><dd>Given a temporary password, which they must change the first time they sign in - unless whoever adds the account types a password, which the person keeps.</dd>
        <dt>Lockout</dt><dd>@SignInThrottle.AccountLimit wrong passwords (or two-step codes) for one account within @((int)SignInThrottle.Window.TotalMinutes) minutes lock it for @((int)SignInThrottle.Window.TotalMinutes) minutes; @SignInThrottle.AddressLimit from one computer block that computer.</dd>
        <dt>Last 7 days</dt><dd>@(Model.RecentLockouts == 0 ? "No lockouts." : $"{Model.RecentLockouts} lockout{(Model.RecentLockouts == 1 ? "" : "s")}.") @if (Model.CanSeeAudit) { <a asp-page="/Settings/Audit" asp-route-section="Sign-in">See the sign-in audit</a> }</dd>
        <dt>Signing out</dt><dd>Ends the session on the server, so a copied sign-in cookie stops working too.</dd>
    </dl>
</section>
